<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annales (Ennius)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annales was an epic poem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hexameters by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Quintus Ennius</a>, written in the early second century BCE. In eighteen books it told the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the flight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> to the poet's own day. It was Rome's national epic until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, and only about 600 lines survive, in quotations by later writers.</p></div>
